From a673f009f28f3723dd171cdb55c9dadfa447b607 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hizuru254 <2654837056@qq.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:06:24 +0800
Subject: [13/14] v1.6 full scan: no pattern beats the flat reference

Seven patterned geometries plus the flat baseline, all run with the equilibrated
protocol (in-run anneal, 80 ps flux warm-up, 150 ps measured, 12 ranks, flux
3 eV/ps) and ranked with the interface-model-free plane estimator:

  W160  1.347e-8 +- 0.57e-9   (0.99x flat)
  flat  1.357e-8 +- 0.62e-9   (1.00x)
  W240  1.357e-8 +- 0.57e-9   (1.00x)
  W080  1.374e-8 +- 0.57e-9   (1.01x)
  D040  1.380e-8 +- 0.64e-9   (1.02x)
  P350  1.403e-8 +- 0.55e-9   (1.03x)
  W200  1.391e-8 +- 0.63e-9   (1.03x)
  D080  1.411e-8 +- 0.64e-9   (1.04x)

The whole design space spans 4.7 %, while a single point carries 4-5 % error:
no groove geometry is distinguishable from the flat reference, let alone better.
The early v1.3 screening trends (W up -> TBR down, P350 best) do not survive
equilibration; they were relaxation-rate differences, not steady-state ones.

Reason: the resistance is dominated by the ~10 A Ti/Au interdiffused layer,
whose conductance is set by the potential, not by the groove shape. Lowering TBR
in this stack needs different interface chemistry, not a different pattern.
